WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.220
- Why do you want to join consulting?

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.870
Why do you want to join this firm?

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.300
Please guide me through your CV.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:08.690
So in consulting interviews,

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.900
questions like this will be often asked.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.490
As, you know, consulting interviews

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:17.017
have also other parts such
as case interview sections.

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:19.913
But these more personal
fit related questions

00:00:19.913 --> 00:00:22.020
definitely are important.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.820
And often people underestimate
how relevant they are.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.060
So this video focuses exactly on that,

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.222
so on questions that are more
related to the personal fit.

00:00:31.222 --> 00:00:33.410
So welcome to another Coffee Break

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.390
here on my channel, Firm Learning.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:38.410
My name is Heinrich, I'm a
former McKinsey consultant,

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.657
and I want to help you
to become successful

00:00:40.657 --> 00:00:43.690
in the first years of your career.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:45.601
So I structured the video in
the following way for you:

00:00:45.601 --> 00:00:49.560
First, I will share some
overarching considerations and tips

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.310
on these personal fit type of questions.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.130
Clearly this will not only
be relevant for consulting,

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.460
but also if you're interviewing
for a corporate job,

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.050
or maybe even a banking job.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.440
And then I'm going to go

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.670
into a couple of very specific questions

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.280
that you will frequently hear

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.180
in these types of interviews.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.970
I'm going to share a couple of tips

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.640
of what you probably should say,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.620
also what you likely should not say.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.770
And I would also provide an answer

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.210
of how I might answer these questions

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.010
in a real interview situation.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.120
And also just to get this
straight in the beginning,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.200
I would never claim

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.379
that I'm the biggest
interview master of all.

00:01:24.379 --> 00:01:26.330
I would never claim that the way

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:28.487
that I would teach this
to you in this video

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:31.179
is the one and only way
you can be successful.

00:01:31.179 --> 00:01:33.048
But I do think that there are
a couple of considerations

00:01:33.048 --> 00:01:35.630
that will make sense for you as well.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.980
So let's think for that
and let's dive into it.

00:01:38.954 --> 00:01:40.735
- Hi, pleasure.

00:01:40.735 --> 00:01:41.810
- Pleasure to be here.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.120
Thank you for inviting me.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:43.970
- Yeah, great.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:44.833
Let's sit down.

00:01:46.586 --> 00:01:49.560
- So let's start with some
overarching reflections

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.950
on personal fit type of questions.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:52.910
And first of all,

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:57.120
I want to talk about a
misconception that I often perceive

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.840
when people talk about this topic,

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.597
and this is the personal fit means

00:02:00.597 --> 00:02:03.895
that every single
consulting firm out there

00:02:03.895 --> 00:02:08.170
has their own type of consultant
that they want to hire,

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:12.090
and they will look whether you
are this very specific type

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:16.020
that fits the criteria of
this very individual firm.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.230
And frankly, I do not
think that this is correct.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:19.267
I do not think that the typical consultant

00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:22.259
that, for instance, McKinsey would hire,

00:02:22.259 --> 00:02:25.120
is very different from
the typical consultant

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.740
that Boston Consulting
Group or Bain would hire.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:28.573
Right?

00:02:28.573 --> 00:02:32.180
I do think that personal
fit is rather homogeneous

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.670
among these firms.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:36.660
I don't think these differences
are that pronounced.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.100
In my mind, personal fit questions
are about something else.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.230
And to better understand this,

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:44.030
try to put yourself into
the shoes of an interviewer

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:47.030
who will often be a
project lead or partner

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:48.600
of that consulting firm.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.130
What do you think would be
important to that interviewer

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:53.630
when the interviewer evaluates a candidate

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.070
with whom he or she
conducts the interview?

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:57.480
So in my opinion,

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.596
the main question that these
people want to answer is,

00:03:00.596 --> 00:03:04.610
what would happen if I take
this potential consultant

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:07.760
with me next week, on my next project,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.400
if I sit him or her in the team room,

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.060
if this person is doing his or her job,

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:17.200
if this person sits in front
of a client for the first time?

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.440
Will this person be able to do the job?

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.640
Will this person be able
to represent him or herself

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.800
and the firm in a great
way in front of the client?

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.760
Or will this not be the case?

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.070
When you answer questions,

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.610
are you perceived as
confident or rather insecure?

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.000
And if you tick all these boxes,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.380
if you really portray yourself as someone

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.130
who could really do this
job, well, then yes,

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.184
you will have the personal
fit to be a consultant

00:03:44.184 --> 00:03:45.630
at one of these firms.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:46.463
And again,

00:03:46.463 --> 00:03:48.700
I don't think that these
criteria very different,

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.520
comparing a firm like McKinsey,

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.810
with Boston Consulting Group, Bain,

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:54.280
or any other of these firms.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:55.997
Let's start with question number one.

00:03:55.997 --> 00:03:58.390
And this is why consulting?

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:00.400
Why do you want to become a consultant?

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.960
Why do you want to join this
career in the first place?

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.840
So what most people likely will answer,

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.864
is talking about the
learning opportunities,

00:04:06.864 --> 00:04:10.040
talking that you're intrigued
about getting to know

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.070
so many different industries,

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:14.260
so many different functions,

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.036
in a rather short amount of time.

00:04:16.036 --> 00:04:16.870
Of course,

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:19.463
I hope that also by watching
videos on this channel or so,

00:04:19.463 --> 00:04:21.270
you understand a bit better

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:23.134
what a consulting career
would be all about,

00:04:23.134 --> 00:04:26.310
and what you might find
fascinating about that,

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:30.170
and sharing exactly that shortly
would not be a better idea.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:31.780
And of course the more personal it is,

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.074
and the less generic it is,

00:04:33.074 --> 00:04:34.810
the more sense it makes.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:35.950
What's always strong here,

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:38.610
is to be able to draw on past experiences.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:39.443
So clearly,

00:04:39.443 --> 00:04:41.810
if you you already did an
internship at a consulting firm,

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.170
it's great to talk about that,

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.390
because this also shows that you do know

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.370
what you're getting yourself into,

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.760
that it's not just a crazy idea
for you to join consulting,

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.690
but that you've really planned this out.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.540
But let's also talk
about a couple of things

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.460
I would not recommend you to say,

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.380
those things that you should rather avoid.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.520
And first thing is about
explaining your motivation

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:02.520
due to the exit opportunities.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.190
So I do not think it would be a great idea

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:06.197
to answer the question, why coinciding,

00:05:06.197 --> 00:05:07.461
by saying something like, oh,

00:05:07.461 --> 00:05:10.940
I heard that if I work a
couple of years in consulting,

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.692
that I can exit, and then
I can have a great career.

00:05:13.692 --> 00:05:16.420
So while it's not a secret,

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:17.530
everybody would understand

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:19.670
that many people leave
after a couple of years,

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:21.560
probably this should not
be your main motivation

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:22.750
to start consulting,

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.695
because then who knows
whether you would enjoy

00:05:25.695 --> 00:05:26.810
the actual job as well.

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.060
A second thing to avoid
is to talk about things

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:30.147
which you really enjoy,

00:05:30.147 --> 00:05:33.640
but which will likely not
be possible in consulting.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:34.730
And this for instance,

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:37.150
is true for people maybe with a PhD,

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:39.550
with many years of experience in academia,

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.440
then in such an interview
say that you really enjoy

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:45.560
working for a long time on a topic,

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:49.510
really understanding it to
the smallest level of detail,

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:51.020
and that this is your passion.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:51.940
I hope you understand,

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.920
if you watched a couple of
videos on this channel that this

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:58.250
just does not resonate with
your reality as a consultant.

00:05:58.250 --> 00:05:59.790
But now let's look into an example

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:02.520
of how you could answer such a question.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.353
- So Heinrich,

00:06:03.353 --> 00:06:06.070
tell me, why do you want
to work in consulting?

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:08.900
- So likely since the
beginning of my studies,

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.650
this career in consulting
really always intrigued me.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:13.480
And what I likely had in mind first,

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.300
is just this learning opportunity,

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.990
of working in so many
different industries,

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:19.496
so many different functions,

00:06:19.496 --> 00:06:22.100
in just such a short amount of time.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:23.950
And I only wanted to just find out

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:25.780
whether this would be
something that I would enjoy,

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:27.453
which is also why after
my second semester,

00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:30.350
when I did an internship in IT consulting.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.489
I worked on a big SAP
implementation project,

00:06:33.489 --> 00:06:35.560
and I did learn a lot of things there.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.210
And among these things that are learned,

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:39.220
I found out really a second thing

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:41.040
that I enjoyed so much about consulting.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.862
And this is really working
together with clients.

00:06:43.862 --> 00:06:46.130
From my reflection so far,

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:48.630
I think there would be very,
very few other careers,

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:50.020
where in such a short amount of time,

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:52.410
you can talk to so many smart colleagues,

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:53.720
but also smart clients,

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:56.610
and really help them solve their problems.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.900
This is what I always found
extremely fascinating.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.500
Now hoping strategy consulting,

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.750
that now I even have the
opportunity to also work more

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:06.870
on more strategic, more
conceptually tasks,

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.770
and projects, which I
would enjoy even more,

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.330
which is why I'm sitting right here today.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.110
Let's move on to the next question.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:15.365
And this is one where
I know that many people

00:07:15.365 --> 00:07:17.590
struggle to answer this a lot.

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:20.090
And this is why this firm?

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:23.950
Why do you want to join this
specific consulting firm?

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:25.320
And people struggle with that,

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.497
because clearly the differences
among consulting firms

00:07:27.497 --> 00:07:30.250
are often not that great,

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:33.340
especially the differences
among firms in the same niche.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:36.500
So for instance, MBB firms,
or maybe the Big Four,

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.100
or other similar firms.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:40.230
So how do you approach this?

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:41.950
And I think there are two ways to do that.

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:44.460
The first, maybe most seem
to be for smaller firms,

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.920
and the other most
suitable for larger firms.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:48.290
And probably then there's a third,

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:50.088
which you can apply to all of these firms.

00:07:50.088 --> 00:07:52.210
So for smaller firms,

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:54.950
these firms offer really
have very specific niche

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:57.810
or focused topic that
they operate in here.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.120
I'm thinking especially about these

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.470
boutique type of consulting firms.

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:04.080
So if you're interested to
work for a pricing boutique,

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:05.010
definitely makes sense

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:07.610
to talk about how passionate
you are about pricing,

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:09.670
and of course, ideally also underline this

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:11.340
with past experiences,

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.730
show them what you already
did in pricing in the past.

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:16.370
Show them, prove them that
this is really your passion,

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:18.330
and that this is really
what you want to do.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:19.280
With larger firms,

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:20.440
what often works a bit better

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:22.850
is to really stress the
opportunities that you have

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:24.230
with this specific firm.

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:25.063
So for instance,

00:08:25.063 --> 00:08:26.380
if you're joining a firm like McKinsey,

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:27.890
or Boston Consulting Group,

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:32.020
you will definitely have a much
larger selection of projects

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:34.630
you can work on, on
topics that you can cover.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:35.610
Now, the third approach,

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:36.860
which I do think can be relevant

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:38.560
for firms of different sizes,

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:40.850
is really to leverage a personal network,

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:42.955
personal connections that
you have to other people

00:08:42.955 --> 00:08:44.040
working in the firm.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:46.130
So clearly it's very strong to say

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:49.010
that you recently talked,
had a long conversation,

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:50.690
with someone working at the firm.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:52.380
Or maybe you attended a career event,

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.990
or maybe one of your
friends was at the firm.

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:58.310
And I'll stress a couple of
things that this person told you

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:01.540
about the firm, about
the very specific culture

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:02.373
of this firm.

00:09:02.373 --> 00:09:04.370
What's something that
you really, really like,

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:05.240
really enjoyed,

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:07.370
because this not only shows
that you did your research,

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:09.253
but clearly you're also already started

00:09:09.253 --> 00:09:11.950
building your network in the form.

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:14.640
And this is always a
great way to do anyways.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:16.110
So what should you not say?

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:17.436
What should you avoid?

00:09:17.436 --> 00:09:20.530
Here I have a candidate of
mine, whom I talked to once.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:22.775
And this person said in the interview

00:09:22.775 --> 00:09:26.550
that his main motivation
to join exactly this firm

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.840
is that he believed that now for instance,

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:31.770
McKinsey is the most
prestigious firm, right?

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:35.550
And this person wanted to join
the most prestigious firm.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:37.800
And this was the main motivation.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:40.530
And clearly this is not the best answer,

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:43.270
because it doesn't really
come out of the values,

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.280
or really the benefits and upsides

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:46.577
that this firm can really provide to you.

00:09:46.577 --> 00:09:48.340
And the fact alone that you say this,

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:50.050
even though you could have said many,

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:51.710
much more insightful things,

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:55.250
also shows that maybe you're
not the smartest person, right,

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:57.340
not understanding that this is likely

00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:59.640
not the best answer to
give in such an interview.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:00.810
So avoid answers like that,

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:01.900
and then you should be fine.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:03.040
But to illustrate that,

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:04.940
let's jump into an example this way.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:07.940
- Why do you want to work
in this specific firm?

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:11.400
So surely every student
considering joining coinciding

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.110
needs to at least think about your firm,

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:15.858
given that you are the
market leader in the segment.

00:10:15.858 --> 00:10:19.400
What then really made
for my decision to apply,

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.500
and to hopefully work here in the future,

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:23.880
is that I talked to a
friend, and also colleague,

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:26.260
who works at the Berlin
office at your firm.

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:28.250
And he taught me a lot about the culture,

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:31.080
what it's like to be a
consultant at your firm.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.088
And one thing that really stuck with me

00:10:33.088 --> 00:10:36.450
is this idea of always the best idea,

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:37.980
the best concept wins.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:39.390
So what I learned is that apparently,

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:41.420
you really have the strong culture

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:43.510
that every single one in the team room,

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:45.557
even the most junior colleagues,

00:10:45.557 --> 00:10:48.270
are expected and encouraged to contribute,

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:49.965
to make their point, to think very hard

00:10:49.965 --> 00:10:53.540
about what the solution to
the client's project would be,

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:55.850
are encouraged to speak up in meetings,

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:57.460
to really already, from day one,

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:00.270
contribute to the problem solving process.

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.430
This is really what I find encouraging.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:04.270
This is what I would love to be

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:06.750
in such an atmosphere of a business,

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:08.440
closely to what it's really like

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:09.660
to work at your firm.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:11.001
I would love to be part of that.

00:11:11.001 --> 00:11:13.650
Last but not least, let's
talk about questions,

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.600
like, can you please
guide me through your CV?

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:18.740
Or can you please introduce yourself?

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:21.610
So these are open questions that ask you

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.060
just to go over your CV,

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:25.870
provide a summary of
what you did in the past,

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:28.320
and how this relates to your current job.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:30.550
Questions like that are
often used in the beginning

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:32.910
of an interview as an icebreaker,

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:35.130
because the interview
will assume that likely,

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:38.110
it's more easy for you just
to talk about yourself,

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.840
about your past experiences,

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:41.810
than directly jumping into a case.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:43.694
So what I would recommend
you to do are two things.

00:11:43.694 --> 00:11:47.490
First, prepare an answer to that ad home.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:50.200
Don't sit there, surprise
by a question like that,

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:52.770
and then just like randomly
talking about a couple of things

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:53.980
which you did in the past,

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:56.910
because of this likely within
come across as rather random,

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:59.570
not very structured,
not very professional.

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:00.403
And second,

00:12:00.403 --> 00:12:02.800
also be open to then
transfer into a conversation

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:03.633
with the interviewer.

00:12:03.633 --> 00:12:05.970
Maybe the interview
will ask you a question,

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:07.540
be then open to answer that,

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:10.360
and then evolve a
conversation based on that,

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:11.877
this really also just shows and stresses

00:12:11.877 --> 00:12:13.719
your interpersonal skills,

00:12:13.719 --> 00:12:16.420
that you can have a good
conversation with someone,

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:18.470
with a collide potentially in the future,

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:20.100
which would also be likely something

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:22.720
that interviewers will want to
see in situations like that.

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:23.560
But once again,

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:26.020
let's jump into an example here as well.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:29.040
- Heinrich, tell me a little
bit more about yourself,

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:30.540
and guide me through your CV.

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:33.680
So I was born in Berlin, but
raised in the West of Germany

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:35.860
in a very rather rural area.

00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:38.050
Then I decided to study
business in Munich,

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.162
probably the main reason being,

00:12:39.162 --> 00:12:42.550
because Munich is just a
city that are always loved,

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:44.700
but of course the business
school is highly ranked

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:45.990
here in Munich, as well.

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:48.230
Let me just talked about
the passion for consulting,

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:50.640
how I was very early on,
intrigued to doing that,

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:52.300
which is why after my second semester,

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:54.292
then I did an internship in IT consulting,

00:12:54.292 --> 00:12:56.909
but then shortly after
also did an internship

00:12:56.909 --> 00:13:00.130
at a Munich-based docs corporation,

00:13:00.130 --> 00:13:02.027
where I worked in their
business development function.

00:13:02.027 --> 00:13:05.100
And this is really also
just such a great area

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.140
to learn about how you can evolve

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:08.760
the strategy of the company,

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:10.767
what our next big
strategic initiatives are.

00:13:10.767 --> 00:13:12.790
This is what I really enjoyed there

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:15.180
which is also part of that
why I think consulting,

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:18.540
strategy consulting, would be
an area where these two things

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:19.490
can come together.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:22.060
Then in the end of my bachelor,
I wanted to do a master,

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:25.054
so applied to some of the big
European business schools.

00:13:25.054 --> 00:13:27.500
I was always intrigued by finance.

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:29.970
This was always a topic
that very interested me

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:31.480
during my undergraduate degree.

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:34.150
I never really was that much into work

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:35.579
in actually investment banking,

00:13:35.579 --> 00:13:38.250
but just the topic of corporate
finance really intrigued me,

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:41.160
which is then why I decided
to do a finance master.

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:42.620
And then I started studying

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:43.920
in the accounting and finance master

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:45.460
at the London School of Economics.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:47.080
I had a great year there,

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:48.780
and everything that I learned there

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:49.900
just encouraged me further

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:51.248
to really work in consulting,

00:13:51.248 --> 00:13:53.440
which is why I'm sitting here right now.

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:54.830
But now let me hear from you.

